    {
      "description": "Explains why this dashboard stops at aggregate solver behaviour and where per-solver data does live.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 9,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 23
      },
      "id": 13,
      "options": {
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "**No metric in this service carries a `solver` label, so there is no per-solver panel.**\n\n`vortex_intent_state_transitions_total` has `from_state` and `to_state` and nothing else.\nAdding `solver` would make cardinality proportional to the size of the solver registry, and\na label whose value set can be grown by whoever registers a solver is a cardinality\nhazard, not a feature. The same reasoning applies to the per-route `topk` panels on the\nAPI dashboard: bounded, or not at all.\n\n**Where per-solver data actually lives**\n\n- `GET /api/v1/solvers/leaderboard` - the on-chain registry ranking, served by\n  `SolversService`. This is the authoritative per-solver view; it is a read of chain\n  state, not an aggregation of this service's counters.\n- The `accepted -> filled` ratio above is the aggregate proxy. Solvers that accept and\n  never fill move it even when total accept throughput looks healthy.\n\n**If a `solver` label is added** to `vortex_intent_state_transitions_total`, add a\n`topk(20, sum by (solver) (...))` panel here, bound the registry size in\n`src/metrics/metrics.service.ts` next to the metric definition, and re-check this\ndashboard against the 3s / 7-day budget before merging.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "There is no per-solver panel here, and that is deliberate",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
